WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.440
So what I'd like to do is to think about
the ways in which there are three

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:10.050
components of urbanism that link the
past and the present. The real building

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:22.100
blocks of urbanism are primarily people,
places, and the resultant possibilities.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:26.730
Even though we might think that the
cities of the ancient world and the

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:31.650
cities of today must have been so very
different because we have electricity

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:38.160
and they didn't, and we have BART and
they didn't, what I can say is that in

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.500
those categories of people, places and
possibilities, modern and ancient cities

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:50.250
were exactly the same. So there are a
number of ways that we can sort of peel

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:56.760
back those layers of time and think
about how urban continuities,

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:03.570
how the idea of being an urban animal, is
something that is now with us and is

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:08.490
going to be with us forever—or at least
the next 10,000 years.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:12.900
So what do modern and ancient
cities have in common? First of all,

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:18.119
there's monumental architecture: that
sense of people building things that are

00:01:18.119 --> 00:01:22.770
much greater than what any particular
family would need, or what any community

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:27.840
would need for any kind of temporary
purpose. What we have is monumental

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:33.780
architecture that is often functional,
sometimes entirely symbolic, like Coit

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.610
Tower or the Eiffel Tower or any of
those other kinds of towers. But it's the

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:44.610
capacity to make and imagine large-scale
structures that really links the past

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:49.290
and the present.
Another thing that cities have in common

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:55.649
all over the world are divisions of
labor. So even though we often think

00:01:55.649 --> 00:01:59.729
about Adam Smith and The Wealth of
Nations as describing how it is that we

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:03.930
can have more efficient production
processes by dividing labor and having

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:08.700
people specialize in tasks, this is
something that smart managers knew 6,000

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:14.630
years ago as well. We can see in the
world that there were firings of kilns

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:23.120
to produce ceramic vessels twenty to
twenty-five thousand pieces at a time. So

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.280
these massive quantities of production
and consumption and also discard is

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:35.750
something that we can clearly see in every
urban settlement past or present. Another

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:40.760
thing that we get in both modern and
ancient cities are the diverse economies

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:45.250
that characterize the migrations of
people coming in, and the specialties of

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:50.390
different kinds of activities that
people are willing to take a chance on. A

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:55.970
long time ago, the archaeologist V. Gordon
Childe talked about how being in a city

00:02:55.970 --> 00:03:01.640
was a godsend for somebody who was
manufacturing something like metal tools

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:06.560
that farmers might need only once or
twice a year. So they couldn't keep

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:12.880
somebody like that in a village, they had
to have this itinerant way of life until

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:18.430
cities came up and made it possible for
somebody to produce something very

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:25.250
utilitarian or even very esoteric and be
able to have customers every single day

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:30.860
coming to them. So this is why cities are
places of innovation. And when you think

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:35.329
about Fashion Week, Fashion Week is
always in a city. When you think about

00:03:35.329 --> 00:03:41.329
tech innovations, they're almost always
in cities, so that that large number of

00:03:41.329 --> 00:03:48.680
producers is met by a large number of
consumers. We also get urban planning as

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:53.650
something that we find in every urban
settlement, past and present. And

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:58.910
oftentimes archeologists have looked
only at kind of the downtown areas of

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:04.910
ancient settlements where that sense of
planning is the most regularized. We're

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:09.790
only now starting to look at some of the
outlying areas where planning is often

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:15.290
escaped by the people who live in them.
So suburban areas and slum areas of

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:19.909
ancient cities are places where we can
see that the urban planning ethos is not

00:04:19.909 --> 00:04:24.520
quite reached, just as we have the same
concerns for our own growing

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:31.030
cities both here in the US and in other
places as well. We get extensive

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:36.490
hinterlands, so that the products that
come into urban settlements might come

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:42.699
from an enormous distance, and yet as a
consumer, you know that when you go to a

00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:47.289
market, you will be able to find what you
need, whether it's an apple from Belgium

00:04:47.289 --> 00:04:52.870
or an apple from New Zealand or an apple
from Shasta County. You know as a

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:58.030
consumer all these pathways of
transportation bring things to you that

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:05.800
you want and need from that diverse and
often distant hinterland. And finally

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:09.460
it's the social networks that make it
possible for people in cities to

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:13.599
interact: to interact with their
neighbors, to interact with their friends

00:05:13.599 --> 00:05:20.310
and also, very importantly, to interact
with strangers.

